The conductivity of an electrolyte solution depends on the nature of the electrolyte and its concentration.
Table salt and sugar are soluble in water. When sugar dissolves in water its molecules remain intact because water can not break apart the strong covalent bonds between their atoms. As a neutral molecule, sugar in solution will not conduct electricity and it is an example of a \emph{nonelectrolyte}. The conductivity of nonelectrolyte solutions is very low. 
For example, at room temperature, the conductivity of ethanol \ce{CH3CH2OH} , a nonelectrolyte is only 1$\mu$S$\cdot $cm$^{-1}$.
Differently, table salt, sodium chloride or \ce{NaCl}, is an ionic compound that gives place to two ions, \ce{Na+} and \ce{Cl-} when dissolved in water. Table salt is a \emph{strong electrolyte}. The conductivity of strong electrolyte solutions is very large. For example, at room temperature, the conductivity of a 0.05M \ce{KCl} solutions, a strong electrolyte just like table salt, is 5000$\mu$S$\cdot $cm$^{-1}$. These ions are responsible for the solution's conductivity. The number or more specifically the concentration, and the mobility of these ions will determine how conductive the solution is.
Solutions of weak electrolytes such as \ce{NH3} present a medium conductivity, higher than that for nonelectrolytes but lower than that for strong electrolytes. For example, at room temperature, the conductivity of a 0.06M acetic acid (\ce{CH3CHOOH}) solution is 1000$\mu$S$\cdot $cm$^{-1}$.
